<h2 id="speed">The 40 Time and What It Actually Means</h2>
<p>Kelce didn't run at the combine itself — he underwent sports hernia surgery in January 2013 and wasn't able to work out in Indianapolis. His 4.61-second 40-yard dash came at his Cincinnati pro day instead. For a tight end at nearly 255 pounds, that's a genuinely strong number; it put him among the faster players at the position in his draft class, ahead of names like Tyler Eifert's 4.68. Speed at that size is exactly what turns a tight end from a possession target into a matchup problem for linebackers and safeties alike.</p>
